.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1148 PM PDT Tue Aug 4 2026
(Tonight through Thursday)

The picture from space this evening shows stratus snaking through
the the region. Earlier model guidance struggled with the onset of
its arrival and to some extent the depth as well. It`s currently
around 1000ft and has the potential to get to 1200ft, with coastal
drizzle and patchy fog possible tonight and into Wednesday morning.
The good news is that this will cool temperatures overnight and
perhaps take a bit longer to retreat to the coast Wednesday morning.
With ridging not as strong and those factors in place, the threat
for Moderate HeatRisk has been pulled back with only a few pockets
found across the interior Central Coast and interior E and S Bay
regions. Some of those pockets include areas above the marine layer,
where temperature recovery won`t be as great. Like the prior shift,
will not be issuing a Heat Advisory for Wednesday.

Wednesday night into Thursday morning, the forecast will be on
repeat. Look for the marine layer and associated stratus to seep
into the region leading to another cloudy night.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1148 PM PDT Tue Aug 4 2026
(Thursday night through next Tuesday)

The ridge starts to build back into the region on Thursday, which
will compress the marine layer to perhaps 500ft for the next few
days. At the surface, winds will have shifted from the south to a
more north/northwesterly flow; however, aloft, guidance shows the
southerly flow holding. Deterministic models and ensembles show a
slug on moisture pushing into our region Thurday-Saturday giving us
PWATs ranging from 0.75-1.0, which could lead to things feeling
humid. In terms of the heat portion of the forecast, it looks to be
on track. Data continues to signal 850mb temps in the 28-30C range,
leading to toastier daytime and overnight temperatures. Highs,
especially for interior areas and those not affected by the marine
layer, will be in the 90s to low 100s while low temperatures there
fall into the upper 60s-70s. Widespread Moderate HeatRisk is
expected, with some pockets of Major HeatRisk on Saturday.

Sunday and Monday, the upper level pattern shows the ridge
flattening some. The latest NBM seems to keep temperatures a tad
warmer on Sunday, with the more notable interior cooling trend
occuring on Monday and Tuesday.

