.AVIATION...
(12Z TAFS)
Issued at 509 AM PDT Wed Aug 5 2026

Strong 500 mb high pressure remains over the area, however there
is also a stationary and weak surface low pressure system over the
CA coastal waters within the marine layer. Counter-clockwise winds
with the surface low is entraining higher surface dewpoint air (water
vapor) from the west, the water vapor upon closing in on the CA
coastline is crossing a cooler gradient in sea surface temperatures.
This is resulting in stratus and mist/fog /MVFR-IFR/ reaching the
coastline and inland on onshore winds. Even under strong high pressure,
the marine layer has deepened to ~ 1800 feet. It`s sharply drier and
warmer aloft at and above the marine layer temperature inversion,
supporting nocturnal radiative cooling to space. Onshore winds are
supported by a 2.4 mb SFO-SAC pressure gradient. It`ll take the
morning for stratus, mist/fog to mix out to VFR.

Wildfire smoke from the Pacific Northwest may also get entrained
into the aforementioned low over the coastal waters today based on
recent high resolution near surface smoke forecasts. If smoke is
drawn into the stratus, this may lower surface visibilities in a
mix of smoke, haze and mist today.

Coastal stratus, mist/fog /MVFR-IFR/ return inland on onshore winds
tonight and Thursday morning.

Vicinity of SFO...Stratus /MVFR/ until 20z today then mixing out
to VFR. Stratus returns late tonight-early Thursday by 10z. Light
and variable wind becoming northwest 10 to 15 knots in the afternoon
and evening.

SFO Bridge Approach...Similar to SFO.

Monterey Bay Terminals...Stratus /IFR/ until late morning then
mixing out to MVFR-VFR. Stratus /IFR/ returns this evening and
overnight. Mainly northwest winds 5 to 15 knots.

