.FIRE WEATHER...
Issued at 1000 AM PDT Wed Aug 5 2026

Marine layer brought relief to lower elevations this morning, but
above the marine layer another nocturnal drying event bringing
single digit RH. Simply put, little to no recovery last night.

Cooler and slightly more humid conditions are expected across the
interior today and Thursday. Despite the "cooldown" temperatures
across the interior will still be in the 90s to low 100s Wed/Thu
with min RH teens. Still expecting an onshore push each
afternoon/evening with breezy to gusty winds leading to a few
hours of elevated fire weather conditions.

High pressure will build back over the district late Thursday into
Friday. As such, the marine layer will become more compressed and
interior heat increasing and humidity decreasing. Peak times of
greatest concern will be each afternoon/evening Sat/Sun. This will
push Energy Release Component (ERC) back up to critical levels.
ERC values have climbed above the 97th percentile within the
Interior Central Coast and Diablo-Santa Cruz (Diablo Mountains and
Santa Cruz Mountains) zones with the Mid Coast to Mendocino Zone
(North Bay) to reach the 97th percentile this week. Large fires
become more frequent after ERC values cross the 90th percentile
with extreme burning conditions expected with ERC values above the
97th percentile. 100 hr fuels continue to respond to the hot and
dry conditions with values reaching critical levels across the
region. Latest intel also shows Live Fuel samplings are also
heading toward critical levels.

Lastly, we`re tracking some high level moisture streaming in from
the south Friday and over the weekend. As of right now, upper
level instability seems limited so high based thunderstorms are
not likely. If anything, some high cirrus is possible with some
higher dew points.

